  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nThrough Sunday: Heat and humidity will be the main concern through \nthe period but local radars were showing isolated showers and \nthunderstorms developing over the CWA. This activity will increase \nover the next several hours with the greatest coverage expected \ngenerally over the northwest half of the CWA. This activity will see \na distinct diurnal trend however, guidance suggests a continuation \nof convection through the night in our northwest. Latest satellite \nimagery/RAP analysis still showed our CWA in the weakness between a \nmid level ridge to our west and to our east. This weakness combined \nwith daytime heating of our moist airmass was leading to the \nconvective development. Where convection has not developed, \ntemperatures were climbing in the 90s and combining with high \nhumidity for heat indices climbing in the lower 100s. Peak heat \nindices will top out near 105F this afternoon across the southern \nhalf of the area. The whole CWA is expected to see peak heat index \nvalues near 105F Sunday along with lower chances for rain than \ntoday. /22/ \n\nBy Monday, the upper trough over our area that has allowed for \ngreater coverage of rain and storms should lift north, resulting in \na broad midlevel high over the region. This means our weather should \nremain largely unchanged day-to-day, with any modification coming \nfrom mesoscale features. As the midlevel high strengthens and \nbecomes centered over our area, rain chances will decrease and \ndangerous heat will increase. With decreasing storm coverage through \nmid week, have introduced an \"elevated\" heat graphic mostly for the \nDelta, but including the Jackson metro as well. There may eventually \nneed to be a corresponding heat advisory which will be evaluated in \nfuture updates. The heat is likely to persist through the period and \nhazard graphics may be adjusted throughout. /SAS/",
